import { ApiCopy } from "../../model"

pub fn copy -> ApiCopy = ApiCopy {
  summary: "Find the Unicode category of one Char, such as letter, number, or symbol",
  purpose: "Use generalCategory to inspect whether A is an uppercase letter or ٣ is a decimal digit. It takes one Char and returns a category such as UppercaseLetter or DecimalNumber. A Char holds exactly one Unicode scalar: a Unicode code-point value excluding the numbers reserved for surrogates. Single quotes, as in 'A', make a Char; double quotes make a String. One visible character can contain several scalars.",
  reading: "let names the input 'A' as letter and its category as category. UppercaseLetter is the value for the uppercase-letter category, so the first line displays that name. The next line compares category with unicode.UppercaseLetter using == and returns True. unicode.UppercaseLetter names one possible category value. The result type UnicodeGeneralCategory holds one of 29 named alternatives, rather than a display string.",
  boundaries: "٣ is an Arabic-Indic decimal digit, giving DecimalNumber. U+0301 is a combining acute accent attached to a preceding character, giving NonspacingMark. U+FEFF is a formatting character, giving Format, and U+10FFFF gives Unassigned. The digits after U+ are a hexadecimal code point. An escape such as \\u{0301} writes the scalar with that number. Unassigned shows that a permitted scalar need not be assigned as an ordinary character. A valid Char is not necessarily printable.",
  rules: "The 29 alternatives, in declaration order, are letters UppercaseLetter (Lu), LowercaseLetter (Ll), TitlecaseLetter (Lt), ModifierLetter (Lm), OtherLetter (Lo); marks NonspacingMark (Mn), SpacingMark (Mc), EnclosingMark (Me); numbers DecimalNumber (Nd), LetterNumber (Nl), OtherNumber (No); punctuation ConnectorPunctuation (Pc), DashPunctuation (Pd), OpenPunctuation (Ps), ClosePunctuation (Pe), InitialPunctuation (Pi), FinalPunctuation (Pf), OtherPunctuation (Po); symbols MathSymbol (Sm), CurrencySymbol (Sc), ModifierSymbol (Sk), OtherSymbol (So); separators SpaceSeparator (Zs), LineSeparator (Zl), ParagraphSeparator (Zp); then Control (Cc), Format (Cf), PrivateUse (Co), Unassigned (Cn). Parentheses contain Unicode abbreviations. Surrogate is absent because Char cannot contain one. Eq supports equality and Show supports display. Ord compares these values in this declaration order, not alphabetical or language-specific order. Each call looks up one scalar in fixed Unicode 17.0.0 tables; it does not scan an entire string.",
  mistake: "A category does not describe the structure of a whole visible character. 'é' is LowercaseLetter, while a similar-looking e followed by a combining accent has two scalars. Trying to write the latter as 'e\\u{0301}' produces SES-P0202 because a Char needs one scalar. To preserve that two-scalar spelling, use the String \"e\\u{0301}\"; to work with visible groups, choose grapheme operations. generalCategory does not decompose or normalize the input.",
}

pub fn comparison -> String =
  "For the narrower TypeScript question ‘is this an uppercase letter?’, the Unicode-property regular expression /\\p{Uppercase_Letter}/u is concise. \\p{...} tests a named Unicode property, u selects Unicode-aware interpretation, and test returns a boolean. These are the same five known one-scalar inputs as the Seseragi example. A gives true; the others give false."

pub fn comparisonBoundary -> String =
  "This compares the uppercase-category question. JavaScript has no standard function returning the complete 29-way category value returned by generalCategory. A false result alone does not tell you whether the input is a number, mark, formatting character, or another category. To return every category, combine category-specific property tests or use Unicode data or a library. JavaScript string also does not enforce one scalar: applying this expression to arbitrary text asks whether it contains a matching character. Property data depends on the engine, so agreement on these selected inputs does not establish agreement for all Unicode values."
